    <section class="hero">
        <h1 class="fade-up fade-up-2">Self-host Parachute</h1>
        <p class="tagline fade-up fade-up-3">Your own box. Your own data. One command, five steps, about ten minutes.</p>
    </section>

    {# ============ MOVEMENT 1 · WHY ============ #}
    <section class="section">
        <h2>Why self-host</h2>

        <p>Parachute is a memory you and any AI share &mdash; and self-hosting means that memory lives on a box <em>you</em> control, not a company's servers. It's the whole point:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Own the box.</strong> The hub and vault run on hardware you rent or already own. No account to be locked out of, no service to shut down under you.</li>
            <li><strong>Own the data.</strong> Your notes sit in a database on your disk, in the open. Your AI reads and writes them directly &mdash; nothing round-trips through us.</li>
            <li><strong>No rent to us.</strong> The software is free and open. You pay only for the box it runs on &mdash; from nothing (your own Mac) to a few dollars a month (a small VPS).</li>
            <li><strong>Export anytime.</strong> Your whole vault exports to portable, git-trackable markdown with one command. Leaving is never a migration project.</li>
            <li><strong>No lock-in, ever.</strong> Start on your laptop to try it, move to an always-on box when you're ready &mdash; or hand the running to <a href="/pricing/">Parachute Cloud</a> later. Same vault, same stack, your choice.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="muted">Rather not run a box at all? <a href="/pricing/">Parachute Cloud</a> is the hosted path &mdash; we run the box, you keep the vault. Everything below is for running it yourself.</p>
    </section>

    {# ============ MOVEMENT 2 · PICK YOUR BOX ============ #}
    <section class="section">
        <h2>Pick your box</h2>

        <p>The same one-line installer sets up all three. What changes is the address you'll open at the end.</p>

        <div class="where-grid">
            <div class="where-card">
                <h3>A server with a public address</h3>
                <p>A fresh <strong>Ubuntu or Debian</strong> VPS: DigitalOcean, Hetzner, EC2. Reachable by your phone and by web AIs like claude.ai, around the clock.</p>
                <div class="code-inline"><code>curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/parachute.sh | bash</code></div>
                <p class="muted">You get HTTPS at <code>https://&lt;your-ip&gt;.sslip.io</code> with no domain. Open ports <strong>80</strong> and <strong>443</strong> first (a Security Group on EC2, a Cloud Firewall on Hetzner; DigitalOcean needs nothing).</p>
            </div>
            <div class="where-card">
                <h3>A Linux box at home</h3>
                <p>A spare machine or a laptop behind your home router. Private to you, and free.</p>
                <div class="code-inline"><code>curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/parachute.sh | bash -s -- --local</code></div>
                <p class="muted"><code>--local</code> keeps it on <code>http://127.0.0.1:1939</code>. Without it the script tries public HTTPS, checks whether it's reachable, and falls back to local if your router doesn't forward ports 80 and 443.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="where-card">
                <h3>Your Mac</h3>
                <p>To try it in a few minutes. It sleeps when your Mac sleeps, so your phone can't reach it while the lid is closed.</p>
                <div class="code-inline"><code>curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/parachute.sh | bash</code></div>
                <p class="muted">Installs <a href="https://bun.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bun</a> if you don't have it, runs under launchd, and stays on <code>http://127.0.0.1:1939</code>.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p class="muted">Apt-based Linux only for now (Ubuntu, Debian); RPM distros aren't supported yet. On a 1&nbsp;GB box, add <code>--no-transcription</code> to skip the ~400&nbsp;MB speech model; you can add it later with <code>parachute vault transcription install</code>.</p>
    </section>

    {# ============ MOVEMENT 3 · FIVE STEPS ============ #}
    <section class="section">
        <h2>Five steps, about ten minutes</h2>

        <ol class="start-flow">
            <li>
                <h3>Run the line for your box</h3>
                <p>Open a terminal on the box (on a server, a root shell; the provider's web console works; on a Mac, your own user, not <code>sudo</code>) and paste the line from above. It takes two or three minutes, most of it downloading. It's safe to re-run. When it finishes you'll see:</p>
<pre class="start-output"><code>==&gt; Done
    Open  https://203.0.113.7.sslip.io  to finish setup in your browser.
    Setup token, for the first screen only:
      parachute-bootstrap-…</code></pre>
                <p class="muted">At home or on a Mac the address is <code>http://127.0.0.1:1939</code>. Keep the token: you'll paste it once.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <h3>Open the address and make your account</h3>
                <p>Open the address from the last step in a browser. At home, use a browser <em>on that box</em>, or forward it to your laptop with <code>ssh -L 1939:127.0.0.1:1939 you@the-box</code> and open <code>http://127.0.0.1:1939</code> there.</p>
                <p>The setup wizard has five steps (Welcome and Account share the first screen):</p>
                <p class="start-wizard">
                    <span class="status-badge status-done">Welcome</span> <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
                    <span class="status-badge status-done">Account</span> <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
                    <span class="status-badge status-done">Vault</span> <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
                    <span class="status-badge status-done">Expose</span> <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
                    <span class="status-badge status-done">Done</span>
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Account:</strong> paste the setup token, then pick a username and password.</li>
                    <li><strong>Vault:</strong> give it a name, or leave it blank for <code>default</code>. It opens with a <strong>Getting Started</strong> note your AI can read.</li>
                    <li><strong>Expose:</strong> keep what's selected; you can change it later.</li>
                    <li><strong>Done:</strong> your connection details. They're also on the vault's admin page any time: <code>&lt;your-address&gt;/vault/default/admin/</code>. Skip its <em>Install Scribe</em> button: transcription is already part of your vault.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>
                <h3>Check it worked</h3>
                <p>On the box:</p>
<pre class="start-output"><code>parachute status    # parachute-vault on 1940, active
parachute doctor    # every check passes, or it names the one fix</code></pre>
            </li>
            <li id="connect-mcp-clients">
                <h3>Connect your AI</h3>
                <p>Every AI connects with one address, your vault's MCP endpoint:</p>
                <div class="code-inline"><code>&lt;your-address&gt;/vault/default/mcp</code></div>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Claude Code</strong> (on the same box or over your network): <code>claude mcp add --transport http parachute &lt;your-address&gt;/vault/default/mcp</code>, then start a new session and run <code>/mcp</code>. The first use opens your hub's sign-in.</li>
                    <li><strong>claude.ai, ChatGPT and other web AIs</strong> add it as a <strong>custom connector</strong>. They connect from their own servers, so they need a public HTTPS address: a server, or <code>parachute expose public</code> (see <a href="#reach">below</a>). A <code>127.0.0.1</code> address won't work for them.</li>
                    <li><strong>A script or a client without sign-in:</strong> mint a token on the vault's <strong>Tokens</strong> page, and set its scope to <strong>write</strong> (the page starts on admin), or run <code>parachute auth mint-token --scope vault:default:write</code> (on an HTTPS address, run <code>parachute auth rotate-operator</code> once first; a known issue). Send it as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>
                <h3>See your first note</h3>
                <p>Ask your AI to <em>&ldquo;jot down that I'm trying Parachute today.&rdquo;</em> Then look:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>On an HTTPS address:</strong> open <a href="https://my.parachute.computer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">my.parachute.computer</a>, choose <strong>Connect your own vault</strong>, and enter <code>&lt;your-address&gt;/vault/default</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>On <code>http://127.0.0.1:1939</code>:</strong> open that address itself. The app is built into your hub. (my.parachute.computer can only open HTTPS addresses.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Then ask your AI to <em>&ldquo;read the Getting Started note and help me set up my parachute.&rdquo;</em></p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    {# ============ MOVEMENT 3b · THE PICTURE ============ #}
    <section class="section">
        <h2>What you just stood up</h2>

        <p>A <strong>hub</strong> on port 1939 signs you in and supervises everything. Your <strong>vault</strong>, the notes your AI reads and writes, sits behind it on 1940; every client reaches it through the hub. The <strong>app</strong> is the hub's front door at <code>/</code>. Voice notes become text inside the vault, on the box.</p>

