                        <div class="mm-mod mm-mod-core">Vault<small>your notes &middot; :1940</small></div>
                        <div class="mm-mod">App<small>the front door at /</small></div>
                        <div class="mm-mod">Surfaces<small>UIs you build</small></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <details style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <summary>DigitalOcean, click by click</summary>
            <ol style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                <li><strong>Create an Ubuntu 24.04 droplet.</strong> In <a href="https://cloud.digitalocean.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cloud.digitalocean.com</a> &rarr; <strong>Create &rarr; Droplets</strong>: image <strong>Ubuntu 24.04 (LTS) x64</strong>, size <strong>$6/mo (1 GB)</strong> for hub + vault (add <code>--no-transcription</code>, or pick $12/mo for on-box transcription), region nearest you.</li>
                <li><strong>Open the droplet's Console.</strong> On its page click <strong>Access &rarr; Launch Droplet Console</strong>: a root terminal in your browser.</li>
                <li><strong>Paste the server line and wait two or three minutes.</strong> It ends with your address and your setup token.</li>
                <li><strong>Open the address,</strong> paste the token, choose your username and password. Then follow steps 3 to 5 above.</li>
            </ol>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">Lost the token? It changes whenever the hub restarts. Show the current one, on the box: <code>curl -s -H 'Accept: application/json' http://127.0.0.1:1939/admin/setup | grep -o 'parachute-bootstrap-[A-Za-z0-9_-]*'</code>.</p>
        </details>

        <details style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
            <summary>Use your own domain</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">In your DNS provider, add an <strong>A record</strong> for the hostname you want (e.g. <code>vault.example.com</code>) pointing at the box's public IPv4. Then run:</p>
            <div class="code-inline"><code>curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/parachute.sh | bash -s -- --domain vault.example.com</code></div>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">Switching later? <code>parachute hub set-origin https://vault.example.com</code> rewrites Caddy, fetches a fresh certificate and restarts your modules; your data and account are untouched. Then re-point your AI clients at the new address.</p>
        </details>

        <details style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
            <summary>Unattended (cloud-init) or an AI doing the install</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">For a box that installs itself at first boot, put the same line in its <strong>cloud-init / User Data</strong>. The setup token lands in the boot log (<code>grep -A1 'Setup token' /var/log/cloud-init-output.log</code>), or read it any time as under <em>Lost the token?</em>:</p>
            <div class="code-inline"><code>#!/bin/bash<br>
curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/parachute.sh | bash</code></div>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">Asking an AI agent to install it? Hand it <a href="/install.md">parachute.computer/install.md</a>: a runbook that drives setup entirely by flags, with the checks that prove it worked.</p>
        </details>
    </section>

    {# ============ MOVEMENT 4 · GROW ============ #}
    <section class="section">
        <h2>Grow from here</h2>

        <p>Your hub is up and your AI is connected. Two moves open the rest: reach it from anywhere, and build a surface of your own over your vault.</p>

        <h3 id="reach">Reach it from anywhere</h3>
        <p>On a server, the one-liner already did this: Caddy with a real Let's Encrypt certificate on your own HTTPS address, no tunnel. At home or on a <strong>Mac</strong>, <code>parachute expose public</code> gives you a public URL: add <code>--cloudflare --domain vault.example.com</code> for a <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/products/tunnel/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Tunnel</a> on your own domain (the apex must already be a Cloudflare zone; the free tier is fine, and <code>expose</code> <em>creates</em> the tunnel for you). Drop <code>--cloudflare</code> for a domain-free <code>*.ts.net</code> URL over <a href="https://tailscale.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tailscale</a> Funnel; or <code>tailscale serve</code> keeps the hub private to your own tailnet.</p>

        <h3>Build a surface</h3>
        <p>When you're ready for something shaped to <em>your</em> brain &mdash; a dashboard, a daily journal, a garden &mdash; build a <strong>surface</strong>: a custom UI over your vault, in your own codebase, no Parachute install needed. <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@openparachute/surface-client" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>@openparachute/surface-client</code></a> gives you OAuth sign-in + the vault API and <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@openparachute/surface-render" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>surface-render</code></a> renders your notes &mdash; a working surface in a few lines, hosted free on GitHub Pages.</p>

        <figure style="margin: 1.5rem 0 0;">
            <div class="surface-shot-frame">
                <img src="/assets/install/unisurface.png" alt="A custom Parachute surface over a personal vault, with the quick-capture panel open and the background dimmed behind it" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1000">
            </div>
            <figcaption class="surface-shot-cap">A personal surface &mdash; &ldquo;a quiet garden for the mind&rdquo;: a custom UI built on top of one's own vault.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="starter-callout" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3>Keep climbing: Vault &rarr; Surface &rarr; Agent</h3>
            <p>Your vault is the core. A <strong>surface</strong> is a UI shaped to how you think. An <strong>agent</strong> acts on the vault for you. Your vault's <strong>Getting Started</strong> note walks your AI through filling it and building your first surface end to end &mdash; just ask your connected AI to <em>&ldquo;read the Getting Started note and help me set up my parachute.&rdquo;</em></p>
            <p>See how the pieces compose in the <a href="/modules/">modules overview</a>, build your own UI with the <a href="/surfaces/">surface guide</a>, and for power moves, <a href="/scripting/">script your vault</a> from bash, Python, or JS &mdash; or host for friends and family with the <a href="/multi-user/">multi-user operator guide</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;"><strong>Staying current:</strong> re-run the line for your box; it updates Parachute in place and keeps your data. <code>parachute upgrade</code> updates the app and other modules. Vault migrations run automatically on the first boot after an update. Back up your whole vault to portable markdown with <code>parachute vault export &lt;dir&gt;</code> (and <code>import</code> back).</p>
    </section>

    {# ============ TROUBLESHOOTING ============ #}
    <section class="section">
        <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

        <details>
            <summary>Something not working?</summary>
            <ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                <li><strong>Claude Code doesn't see the vault.</strong> Start a new session (MCP config loads at session start) and run <code>/mcp</code>. Still missing? Re-run <code>parachute vault mcp-install</code> on the hub machine.</li>
                <li><strong>&ldquo;This box already runs Parachute 0.7.&rdquo;</strong> The installer found an older Parachute and kept it, so nothing you have breaks. Moving a running box to the new version is a separate, deliberate step: <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-core#install" target="_blank" rel="noopener">parachute-core takeover</a>.</li>
                <li><strong>&ldquo;https://&hellip;sslip.io isn't reachable yet.&rdquo;</strong> The installer couldn't reach your box from outside, so it kept you on <code>http://127.0.0.1:1939</code>. On a server, open ports 80 and 443 and re-run; it switches to HTTPS once the address answers. At home, that's expected: use <code>--local</code>, or <code>parachute expose tailnet</code> to reach it from your own devices.</li>
                <li><strong>&ldquo;Wrong bootstrap token.&rdquo;</strong> The hub makes a new one each time it restarts. Show the current one, on the box: <code>curl -s -H 'Accept: application/json' http://127.0.0.1:1939/admin/setup | grep -o 'parachute-bootstrap-[A-Za-z0-9_-]*'</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>The wizard sent you to a sign-in page after the Vault step.</strong> Sign in with the account you just made. Your vault exists; its connection details are at <code>&lt;your-address&gt;/vault/default/admin/</code>.</li>
                <li><strong><code>mint-token</code> says <code>unexpected "iss" claim value</code>.</strong> The hub's address changed after the CLI got its key. Run <code>parachute auth rotate-operator</code>, then mint again.</li>
                <li><strong><code>parachute: command not found</code> in a new shell.</strong> Add Bun to your path: <code>echo 'export PATH="$HOME/.bun/bin:$PATH"' &gt;&gt; ~/.bashrc</code> (on a Mac, <code>~/.zshrc</code>), then open a new shell.</li>
                <li><strong>&ldquo;Blocked &hellip; postinstalls&rdquo; during install.</strong> Run <code>bun pm -g untrusted</code> to see which. If they're only <code>onnxruntime-node</code> and <code>protobufjs</code>, that's expected: they only fetch optional GPU files. Anything else, re-run the install.</li>
                <li><strong>Port 1939 or 1940 already in use.</strong> The hub always uses 1939, with the vault on 1940. Find what holds the port with <code>lsof -i :1939</code> and free it.</li>
                <li><strong>A client can't reach the MCP endpoint.</strong> Confirm the URL ends in <code>/mcp</code> and points at the <em>hub</em> origin (the address you sign in at), not the vault's own port <code>1940</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>&ldquo;Parachute 0.8 needs Bun 1.4 or newer.&rdquo;</strong> The installer updates a Bun it installed itself. For a Bun from Homebrew, run <code>brew upgrade bun</code>, then re-run the line.</li>
                <li>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Claude connector fails right after consent over a Cloudflare tunnel</summary>
                        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">You exposed your hub publicly with a <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/products/tunnel/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Tunnel</a> (<code>parachute expose public --cloudflare</code>) and tried to add your vault to the <strong>Claude Desktop / claude.ai / mobile MCP connector</strong>. OAuth consent succeeds, then Claude reports <em>&ldquo;the integration rejected the credentials&rdquo;</em> or <em>&ldquo;the connection was reverted&rdquo;</em> and never reaches the vault &mdash; while Notes and Claude Code (which use a static token) keep working fine, so it looks like an OAuth bug.</p>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Cause &mdash; this is a Cloudflare edge setting, not a Parachute bug.</strong> Consent runs in your <em>browser</em>, so it passes Cloudflare. But the token exchange (<code>POST /oauth/token</code>) and the MCP calls are <em>server-to-server</em> requests from Anthropic's servers &mdash; and Cloudflare's <strong>Bot Fight Mode</strong> / <strong>Super Bot Fight Mode</strong> / <strong>Browser Integrity Check</strong> (or a Cloudflare Access policy) challenges them with a <code>403</code> &ldquo;Access denied&rdquo; page. Claude gets the 403 instead of a token.</p>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Fix &mdash; in your Cloudflare dashboard for the exposed zone:</strong></p>
                        <ul style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                            <li>Turn <strong>off</strong> Bot Fight Mode / Super Bot Fight Mode (Security &rarr; Bots), <em>or</em> add a <strong>WAF Skip rule</strong> scoped to your exposed hostname (or the API paths <code>/oauth/*</code>, <code>/.well-known/*</code>, <code>/vault/*</code>) that skips Bot Fight Mode, Managed Rules, and the Browser Integrity Check.</li>
                            <li>If <strong>Browser Integrity Check</strong> is on, disable it (or skip it for those paths).</li>
                            <li>If you put the hostname behind <strong>Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust)</strong>, remove that policy &mdash; the hub does its own OAuth, and an outer Access gate blocks the API.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Confirm it's this.</strong> From any non-browser tool, hit your public origin with a non-browser user-agent &mdash; e.g. <code>curl -A python-requests https://&lt;your-host&gt;/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server</code>. If Cloudflare's <code>403</code> &ldquo;Access denied&rdquo; HTML page comes back (rather than a hub response) while a normal browser loads the same URL fine, that's the cause.</p>
                    </details>
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Found something we missed? <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-core/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open an issue</a>.</p>
        </details>
    </section>

    <section class="cta">
        <div class="code-inline" style="margin: 1.5rem auto 0.75rem; max-width: 640px; text-align: left;">
<code>curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/parachute.sh | bash</code>
        </div>
